-- title:
--   Unit-normalised torus measures are coupled along y=1
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $L$ be number fields with $L/K$ finite and Galois, let $\sigma$ be a $K$-automorphism of $L$ such that every $K$-automorphism of $L$ lies in the subgroup generated by $\sigma$, assume $[L:K]$ is prime and $\sigma \neq 1$. Let $v$ be a nonzero prime of $\mathcal{O}_K$, let $a \neq b$ be units of the completion $K_v$, and let $\alpha,\beta$ be units of $L \otimes_K K_v$. Write $\gamma = \mathrm{diag}(a,b) \in GL_2(K_v)$ and $\delta = \mathrm{diag}(\alpha,\beta) \in GL_2(L \otimes_K K_v)$, and let $\iota$ be the homomorphism $GL_2(K_v) \to GL_2(L \otimes_K K_v)$ induced by $x \mapsto 1 \otimes x$. Assume the norm string $\prod_{i=0}^{[L:K]-1} \sigma_{GL}^{i}(\delta)$, where $\sigma_{GL}$ acts entrywise through $\sigma \otimes \mathrm{id}$, equals $\iota(\gamma)$. Let $\tau$ be a Haar measure, for the Borel $\sigma$-algebra of the subspace topology, on the centralizer of $\{\gamma\}$ in $GL_2(K_v)$, with $\tau$-mass $1$ on the set of elements whose matrix and inverse matrix have entries in $\mathcal{O}_{K_v}$; let $\tau'$ be a Haar measure on the twisted centralizer $\{t : t\,\delta\,\sigma_{GL}(t)^{-1} = \delta\}$, with $\tau'$-mass $1$ on the set of elements whose matrix and inverse matrix have entries in the image of $\mathcal{O}_L \otimes \mathcal{O}_{K_v}$ in $L \otimes_K K_v$. Then $\tau$ and $\tau'$ are coupled along $y = 1$: the pushforward of $\tau'$ under $t \mapsto 1^{-1} t \, 1$ equals the pushforward of $\tau$ under $t \mapsto \iota(t)$, as Borel measures on $GL_2(L \otimes_K K_v)$.
--
--   This supplies the measure-matching clause in the local comparison of orbital and twisted orbital integrals for base change along a cyclic extension of prime degree, in the split regular case with both torus measures normalised to give mass one to the integral points. It is used in the identification of the twisted orbital integral of $\mathrm{diag}(\alpha,\beta)$ with the orbital integral of $\mathrm{diag}(a,b)$ and in the resulting formula for the split orbital integral.
